{"key":"bj","num":"03","title":"Benin","blurb":"Every place GeoNames records above 500 people in Benin: 87 towns and villages, grouped by region and ordered by population.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benin","d":"Benin, officially the Republic of Benin, formerly known as Dahomey, is a country in West Africa. It is bordered by Togo to the west, Nigeria to the east, Burkina Faso to the northwest, and Niger to the northeast. The majority of its population lives on the southern coastline of the Bight of Benin, part of the Gulf of Guinea in the northernmost tropical portion of the Atlantic Ocean. The capital is Porto-Novo, and the seat of government is in Cotonou, the most populous city and economic capital. Benin covers an area of 112,622 km2 (43,484 sq mi), and its population in 2026 was estimated to be approximately 15 million. It is a tropical country with an economy heavily dependent on agriculture and the exports of palm oil and cotton.\nFrom the 17th to the 19th centuries, political entities in the area included the Kingdom of Dahomey, the city-state of Porto Novo, and other states to the north. France took over the territory in 1894, incorporating it into French West Africa as French Dahomey. In 1960, Dahomey gained full independence from France. As a sovereign state, Benin has had democratic governments, military coups, and military governments. A self-described Marxist–Leninist state called the People's Republic of Benin existed between 1975 and 1990. In 1991, it was replaced by the multi-party Republic of Benin.\nThe official language of Benin is French, with indigenous languages such as Fon, Bariba, Yoruba and Dendi also spoken. The largest religious group in Benin, as projected for 2020 by the Pew Research Centre in 2026 based on 2010 statistics, is Christianity (53%), followed by Islam (31%) and other faiths including African traditional religions (11%). Benin is a member of the United Nations, the African Union, the Economic Community of West African States, the Organisation of Islamic Cooperation, the South Atlantic Peace and Cooperation Zone, Francophonie, the Community of Sahel–Saharan States, the African Petroleum Producers Association and the Niger Basin Authority.","i":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Armed_women_with_the_King_at_their_head%2C_going_to_war-1793.jpg/500px-Armed_women_with_the_King_at_their_head%2C_going_to_war-1793.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","ic":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armed_women_with_the_King_at_their_head,_going_to_war-1793.jpg","groups":[{"name":"Atlantique","items":[{"n":"Abomey-Calavi","u":"https://www.geonames.org/2395914","p":385755,"t":"city","w":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abomey-Calavi"},{"n":"Godomè","u":"https://www.geonames.org/2394092","p":253262,"t":"city","w":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Godomey"},{"n":"Ouidah","u":"https://www.geonames.org/2392308","p":47616,"t":"town","w":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ouidah"},{"n":"Kouandé","u":"https://www.geonames.org/2393287","p":27197,"t":"town"},{"n":"Allada","u":"https://www.geonames.org/2395635","p":23287,"t":"town"},{"n":"Ganvié","u":"https://www.geonames.org/2394227","p":20000,"t":"town","w":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ganvie"},{"n":"Zinvié","u":"https://www.geonames.org/2390731","p":18157,"t":"town","w":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zinvi%C3%A9"},{"n":"Zé","u":"https://www.geonames.org/2390740","p":16903,"t":"town","w":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Z%C3%A8"},{"n":"Tori-Bossito","u":"https://www.geonames.org/2391101","p":14844,"t":"town","w":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tori-Bossito"},{"n":"Hévié","u":"https://www.geonames.org/2393887","p":13450,"t":"town","w":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/H%C3%A9vi%C3%A9"},{"n":"Hinvi","u":"https://www.geonames.org/2393883","p":3604,"t":"village","w":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hinvi"},{"n":"So-Ava","u":"https://www.geonames.org/2391699","p":0,"t":"village","w":"https://en.wikipedia.